<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<appendix xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ap-cardinality">
  <title>Cardinality</title>
  <section xml:id="sec-cardinality-subsets-real">
    <title>Cardinality</title>
    <p>
      For sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, the equality <m>|A|=|B|</m> means that
      there is a bijection between them. In this case, we say that <m>A</m>
      and <m>B</m> have the same
      <term>cardinality</term><idx>cardinality</idx>. We write <m>|A|
      \le |B|</m> if there is an injection <m>A\to B</m>. A set <m>A</m>
      is <term>countably infinite</term><idx>countably infinite</idx> if
      <m>|A|=|\N|</m>. A set is <term>countable</term><idx>countable</idx>
      if it is either finite or countably infinite.
    </p>

    <p>
      We begin with two standard countable sets that will be useful in later
      proofs.
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-n-plus-zero-countable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The set <m>\N\cup\{0\}</m> is countable.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <proof>
        <p>
          The map <m>f:\N\to \N\cup\{0\}</m>, <m>f(n)=n-1</m>, is bijective,
          with inverse <m>g(m)=m+1</m>. Hence
          <m>|\N\cup\{0\}|=|\N|</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-n-times-two-countable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The set <m>\N\times\{0,1\}</m> is countable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Define <m>F:\N\times\{0,1\}\to\N</m> by <m>F(n,0)=2n</m>
          and <m>F(n,1)=2n-1</m>. This map is bijective, so
          <m>|\N\times\{0,1\}|=|\N|</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <p>
      The next proposition shows that <m>\N</m> has the smallest infinite
      cardinality.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-min-countably-infinite">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A set <m>A</m> is infinite if and only if <m>|\N|\le |A|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>f:\N\to A</m> is injective, then <m>f(\N)</m> is an
          infinite subset of <m>A</m>, so <m>A</m> is infinite. Conversely,
          suppose that <m>A</m> is infinite. Choose <m>a_1\in A</m>, and
          recursively choose
          <m>a_{n+1}\in A\setminus\{a_1,\ldots,a_n\}</m>. This is possible
          at every stage because <m>A</m> is not finite. The map
          <m>n\mapsto a_n</m> is an injection from <m>\N</m> to <m>A</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <p>
      Our main tool for comparing cardinalities is the following theorem.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-cantor-bernstein-schroder">
      <title>Cantor-Bernstein-Schroder</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>|A|\le |B|</m> and <m>|B|\le |A|</m>, then
          <m>|A|=|B|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <p>
      Proofs of <xref ref="thm-cantor-bernstein-schroder"/> can be found in
      <xref ref="biblio-garrett-csb"/> and <xref ref="biblio-wikipedia-csb"/>.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="inf_subset_of_N">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Every infinite subset of <m>\N</m> is countably infinite.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an infinite subset of <m>\N</m>. The inclusion
          <m>A\hookrightarrow\N</m> is injective, so <m>|A|\le|\N|</m>.
          Since <m>A</m> is infinite, <xref
          ref="prop-min-countably-infinite"/> gives
          <m>|\N|\le|A|</m>. Therefore <m>|\N|=|A|</m> by <xref
          ref="thm-cantor-bernstein-schroder"/>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      A direct proof of the preceding proposition that does not invoke
      <xref ref="thm-cantor-bernstein-schroder"/> is left as an exercise.
    </p>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-countable-equiv">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be nonempty. The following statements are equivalent:
        </p>
        <list>
          <title>Equivalent statements</title>
          <ol>
            <li xml:id="prop-countable-equiv-countable">
              <p><m>A</m> is countable.</p>
            </li>
            <li xml:id="prop-countable-equiv-injection">
              <p>There is an injection from <m>A</m> to <m>\N</m>.</p>
            </li>
            <li xml:id="prop-countable-equiv-surjection">
              <p>There is a surjection from <m>\N</m> to <m>A</m>.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </list>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          (<xref ref="prop-countable-equiv-countable"/> implies <xref
          ref="prop-countable-equiv-injection"/>.) If <m>A</m> is countably
          infinite, any bijection from <m>A</m> to <m>\N</m> is an
          injection. If <m>A=\{a_1,\ldots,a_k\}</m> is finite, the map
          <m>a_j\mapsto j</m> is injective.
        </p>
        <p>
          (<xref ref="prop-countable-equiv-injection"/> implies <xref
          ref="prop-countable-equiv-surjection"/>.) Let
          <m>i\colon A\to\N</m> be injective, and choose <m>a_0\in A</m>.
          Define <m>s:\N\to A</m> by <m>s(n)=i^{-1}(n)</m> when
          <m>n\in i(A)</m> and by <m>s(n)=a_0</m> otherwise. Every element
          of <m>A</m> has the form <m>i^{-1}(n)</m> for some
          <m>n\in i(A)</m>, so <m>s</m> is surjective.
        </p>
        <p>
          (<xref ref="prop-countable-equiv-surjection"/> implies <xref
          ref="prop-countable-equiv-countable"/>.) Suppose that
          <m>s\colon\N\to A</m> is surjective. Assign to each
          <m>a\in A</m> its least preimage:
          <m>i(a)=\min\{n\in\N:s(n)=a\}</m>. The resulting map
          <m>i:A\to\N</m> is injective and hence gives a bijection from
          <m>A</m> to <m>i(A)</m>. If <m>i(A)</m> is finite, then
          <m>A</m> is finite. If <m>i(A)</m> is infinite, then <xref
          ref="inf_subset_of_N"/> shows that <m>i(A)</m> is countably
          infinite, and therefore so is <m>A</m>. In either case, <m>A</m>
          is countable.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <proposition xml:id="prop-n-square-countable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The set <m>\N^2</m> is countable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Define <m>P:\N^2\to\N</m> by
          <m>P(j,m)=2^{j-1}(2m-1)</m>. Every <m>k\in\N</m> has a unique
          factorization <m>k=2^r q</m>, where <m>r\geq0</m> and <m>q</m>
          is odd. Thus <m>k=P(j,m)</m> for the unique pair
          <m>j=r+1</m> and <m>m=(q+1)/2</m>. Therefore <m>P</m> is
          bijective.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      See <xref ref="ex-n-square-explicit-bijection"/> for an alternative
      constructive proof based on diagonal enumeration.
    </p>

    <p>
      The countability of <m>\N^2</m> yields an important closure property.
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-countable-union-countable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A countable union of countable sets is countable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Let <m>A_1,A_2,\ldots</m> be a countable family indexed by
          <m>\N</m>. For each
          <m>a\in\bigcup_{j\in\N}A_j</m>, let <m>j(a)</m> be the least
          index such that <m>a\in A_{j(a)}</m>. For each <m>j</m>, choose
          an injection <m>f_j:A_j\to\N</m>. Then
          <m>a\mapsto(f_{j(a)}(a),j(a))</m> is an injection from
          <m>\bigcup_{j\in\N}A_j</m> into <m>\N^2</m>. Since
          <m>\N^2</m> is countable, the union is countable.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>

    <p>
      We now apply these countability results to the rational numbers.
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-q-countable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The set <m>\Q</m> is countable; consequently, <m>\Z</m> is
          countable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The map <m>n\mapsto n/1</m> is an injection from <m>\N</m> to
          <m>\Q</m>, so <m>|\N|\le|\Q|</m>. For the reverse inequality,
          write each <m>r\in\Q</m> uniquely as <m>r=m/n</m> in lowest terms,
          with <m>m\in\Z</m> and <m>n&gt;0</m>, and map <m>r</m> to
          <m>(m,n)\in\Z\times\N</m>. This map is injective. The map
          <m>c:\Z\to\N</m> defined by
          <m>c(m)=2m+1</m> for <m>m\geq0</m> and
          <m>c(m)=-2m</m> for <m>m&lt;0</m> is also injective. Hence
          <m>(m,n)\mapsto(c(m),n)</m> injects
          <m>\Z\times\N</m> into <m>\N^2</m>. By <xref
          ref="prop-n-square-countable"/>, <m>\Z\times\N</m> is countable,
          and therefore <m>|\Q|\le|\N|</m>. It follows that
          <m>|\Q|=|\N|</m> by <xref
          ref="thm-cantor-bernstein-schroder"/>. Finally, restricting such
          a bijection to <m>\Z\subseteq\Q</m> gives an injection from
          <m>\Z</m> to <m>\N</m>, so <m>\Z</m> is countable.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <p>
      We now turn to uncountable sets.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <m>X</m> be a set and <m>A\subseteq X</m>. The
      <term>indicator function</term><idx>indicator function</idx> of
      <m>A</m> in <m>X</m>, denoted by <m>1_A^X</m> (or simply
      <m>1_A</m> when <m>X</m> is understood), is the map
      <m>1_A^X:X\to\{0,1\}</m> defined by
      <md>
        1_A^X(x)=
            \begin{cases}
              1,&amp;\text{if }x\in A,\\ 0,&amp;\text{if }x\notin A.
            \end{cases}.
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Indicator functions give a bijection
      <m>\mathcal{P}(X)\cong 2^X</m>, where <m>2^X</m> denotes the set of
      maps from <m>X</m> to <m>\{0,1\}</m>.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-cantor-power-set">
      <title>Cantor</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For every set <m>X</m>, <m>|X| &lt; |2^X|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          The map <m>x\mapsto 1_{\{x\}}</m> is an injection from <m>X</m>
          to <m>2^X</m>. To prove that no surjection exists in the opposite
          direction, suppose that <m>\Phi:X\to2^X</m> is surjective. Define
          <m>d:X\to\{0,1\}</m> by <m>d(x)=1-\Phi(x)(x)</m>. Surjectivity
          gives <m>d=\Phi(a)</m> for some <m>a\in X</m>. Evaluating at
          <m>a</m> yields
          <m>\Phi(a)(a)=1-\Phi(a)(a)</m>, a contradiction. Hence
          <m>|X|&lt;|2^X|</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    <corollary xml:id="cor-r-uncountable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The real line <m>\R</m> is uncountable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          By <xref ref="thm-cantor-power-set"/>,
          <m>|\N|&lt;|2^{\N}|</m>. Define <m>\Psi:2^{\N}\to\R</m> by
          <m>\Psi((a_k)_{k\ge1})=\sum_{k\ge1}a_k/10^k</m>. If <m>a\ne
          b</m>, let <m>n</m> be the first index with <m>a_n\ne b_n</m>.
          Then <m>|\Psi(a)-\Psi(b)|\ge 10^{-n}(1-1/9)&gt;0</m>, so
          <m>\Psi</m> is injective. Therefore
          <m>|2^{\N}|\le|\R|</m>. Since
          <m>|\N|&lt;|2^{\N}|</m>, the real line cannot be countable.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </corollary>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-irrationals-uncountable">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The irrational numbers <m>\R\setminus\Q</m> are uncountable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          If <m>\R\setminus\Q</m> were countable, then
          <m>\R=(\R\setminus\Q)\cup\Q</m> would be a union of two countable
          sets and therefore countable, contradicting <xref
          ref="cor-r-uncountable"/>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </section>


  <exercises xml:id="sec-cardinality-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-n-square-explicit-bijection">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>\N^2</m> is countable by constructing an explicit
          bijection from <m>\N</m> to <m>\N^2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          For <m>r\geq0</m>, let <m>T_r=r(r+1)/2</m> be the <m>r</m>-th
          triangular number. Given <m>k\in\N</m>, set
          <md>
            r=r(k)=\left\lceil\frac{\sqrt{8k+1}-1}{2}\right\rceil
            \quad\text{and}\quad
            j=j(k)=k-T_{r-1}.
          </md>
          Then <m>T_{r-1}&lt;k\leq T_r</m>, so <m>1\leq j\leq r</m>.
          Define <m>F:\N\to\N^2</m> by
          <md>
            F(k)=(r-j+1,j).
          </md>
          The map <m>F</m> counts the lattice points one diagonal at a time:
          <md>
            (1,1),(2,1),(1,2),(3,1),(2,2),(1,3),\ldots.
          </md>
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-n-square-diagonal-counting">
          <caption>The diagonal counting route. At each lattice point, the
          label <m>k</m> identifies the point <m>F(k)</m>.</caption>
          <image width="58%">
            <shortdescription>Positive lattice points numbered 1 through 15
            along diagonals, with arrows showing the counting route.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05, &gt;=stealth]
  \draw[step=1, gray!28, very thin] (0.75,0.75) grid (5.25,5.25);
  \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0.6,0.65) -- (5.55,0.65) node[right] {$m$};
  \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0.65,0.6) -- (0.65,5.55) node[above] {$n$};
  \foreach \x in {1,...,5}
    \node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] at (\x,0.65) {$\x$};
  \foreach \y in {1,...,5}
    \node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] at (0.65,\y) {$\y$};
  \foreach \xa/\ya/\xb/\yb in {
    1/1/2/1, 2/1/1/2,
    1/2/3/1, 3/1/2/2, 2/2/1/3,
    1/3/4/1, 4/1/3/2, 3/2/2/3, 2/3/1/4,
    1/4/5/1, 5/1/4/2, 4/2/3/3, 3/3/2/4, 2/4/1/5}
    \draw[-&gt;, blue!70!black, thick, shorten &lt;=5pt, shorten &gt;=5pt]
      (\xa,\ya) -- (\xb,\yb);
  \foreach \x/\y/\k in {
    1/1/1,
    2/1/2, 1/2/3,
    3/1/4, 2/2/5, 1/3/6,
    4/1/7, 3/2/8, 2/3/9, 1/4/10,
    5/1/11, 4/2/12, 3/3/13, 2/4/14, 1/5/15}
    \node[circle, draw=blue!70!black, fill=white, inner sep=1.4pt,
      font=\scriptsize] at (\x,\y) {$\k$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
          To verify that <m>F</m> is bijective, define
          <m>G:\N^2\to\N</m> by
          <md>
            G(m,n)=T_{m+n-2}+n.
          </md>
          If <m>F(k)=(m,n)</m>, then <m>m+n-1=r</m> and <m>n=j</m>, so
          <m>G(F(k))=T_{r-1}+j=k</m>. Conversely, given
          <m>(m,n)\in\N^2</m>, set <m>r=m+n-1</m> and
          <m>k=G(m,n)</m>. Since <m>1\leq n\leq r</m>,
          <m>T_{r-1}&lt;k\leq T_r</m>, and therefore
          <m>F(k)=(r-n+1,n)=(m,n)</m>. Thus <m>F</m> and <m>G</m> are
          inverses, so <m>F</m> is a bijection and <m>\N^2</m> is
          countably infinite.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-subset-of-countable-set">
      <statement>
        <p>Show that any subset of a countable set is countable.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-open-unit-interval-real-bijection">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>\R</m> have the same cardinality by
          constructing an explicit bijection.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-intersection-two-intervals">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the intersection of two intervals is empty, a singleton,
          or an interval.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-union-overlapping-intervals">
      <statement>
        <p>Show that the union of two overlapping intervals is an interval.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</appendix>
